$DIR/main.p:17:2: the field balance of a synced[Account] is written here with no lock that guards it, while another task may use it
    the field balance of a synced[Account] is written at main.p:17 in
    (*Bank).Deposit by the main task with no lock held, and another task started
    there writes it at main.p:17 in (*Bank).Deposit. Two tasks may run these at
    the same time, one of them writing, which is a data race: the other may see
    a value half written, or lose an update.
    The compiler infers which lock guards shared data from the locks held where
    it is written: a lock that every write holds. Here no one lock is held at
    every write.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Hold one sync.Mutex at every access, writes and reads (a sync.RWMutex's
    RLock is enough for reads):
        mu.Lock()
        s.f = v
        mu.Unlock()
    Or, for a number, use an atomic type from pluto/sync/atomic, such as
    atomic.Int64, and its methods at every access.
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3502" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:17:2: b.accs.balance is written by the tasks started at main.p:25 at the same time as each other
    Each call of s.Task at main.p:25 starts a task, and they run at the same
    time. Each writes b.accs.balance (main.p:17, in (*Bank).Deposit called at
    main.p:25), and another reads it (main.p:17, in (*Bank).Deposit called at
    main.p:25). Two tasks using one place at once, one of them writing, is a
    data race: an update can be lost, and a value larger than a word can be read
    half written.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Lock a mutex around every use of b.accs.balance:
        mu.Lock()
        ...
        mu.Unlock()
    Or, for a number, use an atomic type such as atomic.Int64.
    Or give each task its own element: results[i] = ..., where i is the loop
    variable.
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3402" for a longer explanation)
